529
00:30:25,580 --> 00:30:31,820
So first I wanted to talk a little bit about how Grok Bot came to be.

530
00:30:31,820 --> 00:30:36,020
And I think we're in a pretty interesting moment right now where when you think about the way

531
00:30:36,020 --> 00:30:41,620
that AI has changed and really transformed the way that people and companies do work,

532
00:30:41,620 --> 00:30:44,740
I think we've been through a few different phases.

533
00:30:44,740 --> 00:30:50,840
And I think it started with really chat, where you ask a question to AI, AI responds, and

534
00:30:50,840 --> 00:30:51,960
that was really useful.

535
00:30:51,960 --> 00:30:55,800
I think that helped with a bunch of research workflows, for example.

536
00:30:55,800 --> 00:30:59,840
I think the next step after that was you delegate a task to AI.

537
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:03,480
And there's a co-pilot that kind of sits next to you while you do your work.

538
00:31:03,480 --> 00:31:08,600
You iterate on a slide deck together, those types of workflows.

539
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:12,800
But I think we're really on the cusp of this new moment in AI,

540
00:31:12,800 --> 00:31:18,280
where AI starts to act more like a teammate or more like a colleague inside of a company.

541
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:20,640
And that's really different, I think, from what's come before.

542
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:24,920
And I think it's going to change the types of products that really meet the moment,

543
00:31:24,920 --> 00:31:29,800
I think the ways that we need to change our ways and how we do work day to day to really

544
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:34,200
maximally get the most out of what AI can do inside of a company.

545
00:31:34,200 --> 00:31:39,960
And then I think what will come after that is kind of this weird world of AI teammates

546
00:31:39,960 --> 00:31:43,480
and human teammates that collaborate together inside of companies.

547
00:31:43,480 --> 00:31:47,720
And where you're not just delegating a discrete task and saying, hey, go from point

548
00:31:47,720 --> 00:31:52,920
A to point B, you're actually doing a lot more kind of what you would do with an actual

549
00:31:52,920 --> 00:31:56,720
human colleague if they're not a collection of tasks that you kind of outsource,

550
00:31:56,720 --> 00:31:59,840
but they own outcomes and they have responsibilities.

551
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:07,160
And you trust them to kind of own a discrete part of a company and kind of do that work for you.

552
00:32:07,160 --> 00:32:10,800
And I think this is really where Grokbot originated from.

553
00:32:10,800 --> 00:32:15,000
And I want to talk a little bit about some of the product decisions that we made to get there.

554
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:18,280
So if you haven't used Grokbot, this is what it looks like.

555
00:32:18,280 --> 00:32:23,440
And I think there are a few things that we chose to do with Grokbot that are a bit different

556
00:32:23,440 --> 00:32:29,080
from other AI tools on the market that you maybe have used before or are familiar with.

557
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:34,200
I think the first one is we really wanted Grokbot to feel a lot like you're working

558
00:32:34,200 --> 00:32:36,240
with teammates and colleagues.

559
00:32:36,240 --> 00:32:42,960
And so as a result, it feels kind of simple and chat-like and almost like you're messaging

560
00:32:42,960 --> 00:32:44,800
your coworkers or teams of colleagues.

561
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:50,400
So you have on the left all of these bots, in this case, Sales Outbound and an inbox manager,

562
00:32:50,400 --> 00:32:54,600
that each have specific roles and different kind of lanes of work.

563
00:32:54,600 --> 00:32:58,480
And the idea is not that you create a chat for each task that you want to take on.

564
00:32:58,480 --> 00:33:04,240
You actually create a bot for a unit of work and a kind of job responsibility,

565
00:33:04,240 --> 00:33:08,280
and you come back to that bot over and over and over again and it learns from you.

566
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:11,960
And I think that's one of the most magical moments of working with Grokbot is you say

567
00:33:11,960 --> 00:33:15,840
something a few weeks ago about, you know, I really like when my slides are formatted

568
00:33:15,840 --> 00:33:16,840
in this particular way.

569
00:33:16,840 --> 00:33:17,840
And then Grokbot remembers.

570
00:33:17,840 --> 00:33:21,440
It remembers weeks later when you're making your next slide deck.

571
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:24,560
And that's what you would expect from a colleague, and I think that's what you should expect

572
00:33:24,560 --> 00:33:25,920
from AI as well.

573
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:30,440
So there's this teammate paradigm instead of a task paradigm.

574
00:33:30,440 --> 00:33:35,040
Teammates really sit at the center of Grokbot and the way to work with AI.

575
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:39,400
The second thing that's a little bit different is Grokbot, similar to what you would expect

576
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:46,360
from a colleague, doesn't just have access to direct integrations and MCPs and APIs.

577
00:33:46,360 --> 00:33:51,080
Those are very useful, but they oftentimes don't get you 100% of the way to completing

578
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:52,460
the work that you want.

579
00:33:52,460 --> 00:33:57,640
They have access to their own computer, and they can use that computer exactly how you

580
00:33:57,640 --> 00:33:58,640
would.

581
00:33:58,640 --> 00:33:59,640
They can watch a video.

582
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:00,920
They can listen to a podcast.

583
00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:07,200
They can click in some, you know, antiquated government software from the 90s.

584
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:11,800
They can do everything that a human can do with a computer, but it's yours.

585
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:16,520
And in addition to all of the integrations and MCPs and things that you come to know

586
00:34:16,520 --> 00:34:22,320
and love with AI, I think it really opens up a new set of tasks and opportunities for

587
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:24,280
what AI can take off of your plate.

588
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:28,320
And I think it feels really different when you work with AI that can do those things

589
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:29,320
versus can't.

590
00:34:29,320 --> 00:34:34,000
And the difference between getting kind of 90% of the way there of like almost having

591
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:38,240
the job be done versus the thing just being done end-to-end, kind of hands off.

592
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:40,440
You trust that your bot can do it.

593
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:41,440
Feels very different.

594
00:34:41,440 --> 00:34:42,440
Feels really magical.

595
00:34:42,440 --> 00:34:45,720
And then the last thing is everything runs in the cloud.

596
00:34:45,720 --> 00:34:47,720
It doesn't run on your computer.

597
00:34:47,720 --> 00:34:51,520
And I think there's kind of in this weird moment in AI right now where a lot of the

598
00:34:51,520 --> 00:34:57,000
workflows that people rely so much on AI for, it runs on their local laptop, which

599
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:58,420
is kind of strange.

600
00:34:58,420 --> 00:35:02,760
And so if you're like asleep and your computer's closed, you know, the automation that you

601
00:35:02,760 --> 00:35:08,080
set up to run at 2 AM doesn't fire and you wake up in the morning and you're confused

602
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:12,120
and you want to kick something off from your phone but it's tethered to your laptop back

603
00:35:12,120 --> 00:35:14,160
in your office, it's just all this weirdness.

604
00:35:14,160 --> 00:35:19,080
And we wanted to just jump right past that to a future where you have these very smart

605
00:35:19,080 --> 00:35:23,960
colleagues, these teammates, these bots that live in the cloud and you can interact

606
00:35:23,960 --> 00:35:25,440
with them anywhere you work.

607
00:35:25,440 --> 00:35:27,600
It might be on your phone, it might be on your laptop.

608
00:35:27,600 --> 00:35:30,620
In the future, it might be something entirely different.

609
00:35:30,620 --> 00:35:35,100
And that really informed all of the decisions we made for the product, was those three things.

610
00:35:35,100 --> 00:35:40,060
Feels like interacting with teammates, everything runs in the cloud, and your bots have access

611
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:43,740
to a computer that they can drive and manipulate just like you can.

612
00:35:43,740 --> 00:35:49,100
And I think part of the reason we really felt the desire and the excitement to build

613
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:52,660
something like RockBot is from what we've seen in coding.

614
00:35:52,660 --> 00:35:58,700
And I think from the past two years, we've seen the job of a developer completely transform.

615
00:35:58,700 --> 00:36:04,420
And I think a very stark, it's hard to overestimate how big the change has been.

616
00:36:04,420 --> 00:36:10,240
From an era of you're writing individual lines of code by hand to I think what a lot of development

617
00:36:10,240 --> 00:36:15,260
looks like now, certainly inside of SpaceX AI, where you have developers that own outcomes,

618
00:36:15,260 --> 00:36:20,900
they own direction, and they steer their team of agents to complete these discrete tasks

619
00:36:20,900 --> 00:36:24,020
and do kind of a lot of the mundane work for them.

620
00:36:24,020 --> 00:36:26,420
And we haven't seen that for knowledge work at all.

621
00:36:26,420 --> 00:36:30,100
I think when you look at the way that knowledge work is done today versus the way it's done

622
00:36:30,100 --> 00:36:34,380
in 2024 or 2025, it's not that different.

623
00:36:34,380 --> 00:36:40,060
And I think our hope and goal for RockBot is to really be the most useful way to harness

624
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:44,740
these models inside of a company to do real work.

625
00:36:44,740 --> 00:36:49,500
And so I touched on some of these points, but Amrita will talk a little bit more about

626
00:36:49,500 --> 00:36:53,900
specific ways that we're seeing RockBot be useful for us internally, but also inside

627
00:36:53,900 --> 00:36:55,820
of real companies in the wild.

628
00:36:55,820 --> 00:37:02,220
But we wanted it to be delightfully simple, and we think the future of AI products really

629
00:37:02,220 --> 00:37:07,100
should be very simple to use, very simple to get started on, and then gradually pull

630
00:37:07,100 --> 00:37:10,180
you into the more complex capabilities.

631
00:37:10,180 --> 00:37:13,620
And I think a lot of people's experience using RockBot is immediate on its face.

632
00:37:13,620 --> 00:37:18,100
You kind of give it an initial task that is a little straightforward and simple.

633
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:19,100
RockBot does it.

634
00:37:19,100 --> 00:37:20,820
You give it something more ambitious.

635
00:37:20,820 --> 00:37:21,820
RockBot does it.

636
00:37:21,820 --> 00:37:24,580
You give it something even more ambitious, and you just kind of keep going through that

637
00:37:24,580 --> 00:37:29,580
process until you feel the limits of maybe where current AI capabilities are.

638
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:33,420
And our bet is these models are going to keep getting smarter.

639
00:37:33,420 --> 00:37:39,660
We want to give them a safe environment to run in to really maximally be useful to employees

640
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:40,660
of companies.

641
00:37:40,660 --> 00:37:42,180
So it's very simple.

642
00:37:42,180 --> 00:37:43,820
It's always on.

643
00:37:43,820 --> 00:37:47,860
It's this teammate in the cloud that you can always depend on and rely on and never

644
00:37:47,860 --> 00:37:51,700
goes down even when you're asleep or when your computer is closed.

645
00:37:51,700 --> 00:37:53,420
And it really finishes the work.

646
00:37:53,420 --> 00:37:54,420
You give it outcomes.

647
00:37:54,420 --> 00:37:55,420
