6485
05:54:39,390 --> 05:54:40,750
Some of the tips, the tools,

6486
05:54:41,470 --> 05:54:46,990
the bots that we have found very useful across our product org.

6487
05:54:46,990 --> 05:54:49,470
And so I wanted to start with the backdrop,

6488
05:54:49,470 --> 05:54:52,190
which I think everybody in this room on the stream knows.

6489
05:54:52,190 --> 05:54:54,990
If you have been building software, if you're a PM,

6490
05:54:54,990 --> 05:54:58,190
obviously the craft is changing very rapidly.

6491
05:54:58,830 --> 05:55:03,950
It becomes now very easy to code, to build things, to prototype.

6492
05:55:03,950 --> 05:55:07,150
Way more accessible than it ever has been before.

6493
05:55:07,150 --> 05:55:09,710
And I like this quote from DHH.

6494
05:55:09,710 --> 05:55:14,030
The developer's developer, one of the creators of Ruby on Rails,

6495
05:55:14,030 --> 05:55:16,350
who says, software is product management.

6496
05:55:16,910 --> 05:55:17,870
What should it do?

6497
05:55:17,870 --> 05:55:19,230
Who should it do it for?

6498
05:55:19,230 --> 05:55:20,110
How should it do it?

6499
05:55:20,990 --> 05:55:22,270
How should it look?

6500
05:55:22,270 --> 05:55:23,310
What are the priorities?

6501
05:55:23,310 --> 05:55:26,190
These are questions that now every product builder,

6502
05:55:26,190 --> 05:55:31,470
whether you're formally engineer or designer or founder or product manager,

6503
05:55:31,470 --> 05:55:37,950
really has to grapple with as you start to build software for your customers, for your users.

6504
05:55:38,780 --> 05:55:39,180
Yeah.

6505
05:55:39,180 --> 05:55:43,420
So with that, we thought we'd spend just a second talking about the motivation behind

6506
05:55:43,420 --> 05:55:47,260
RockBot because in some ways, RockBot is the product we've also made.

6507
05:55:47,260 --> 05:55:50,060
And so there's a story to it having been built,

6508
05:55:50,060 --> 05:55:53,180
just the same way there's a story to every product having been built.

6509
05:55:53,180 --> 05:55:58,780
And I think if you think back to sort of what motivated RockBot in the early, early days,

6510
05:55:58,780 --> 05:56:02,060
it was realizing that, you know, at least internally within SpaceX AI,

6511
05:56:02,060 --> 05:56:06,620
there are so many folks who were starting to evolve beyond a chat box.

6512
05:56:06,620 --> 05:56:08,540
And I think there's been a lot of discourse about this,

6513
05:56:08,540 --> 05:56:11,100
maybe some of you all have felt this or read about it as well.

6514
05:56:12,140 --> 05:56:18,620
But really, it started to feel increasingly that agents weren't quite as home in a chat box as we

6515
05:56:18,620 --> 05:56:19,660
might have wanted them to be.

6516
05:56:19,660 --> 05:56:23,260
I think a lot of this was just a natural evolution of how we started interacting

6517
05:56:23,260 --> 05:56:28,380
with these large language models and sort of, you know, going from asking for a response

6518
05:56:28,380 --> 05:56:32,300
and maybe waiting for some text to asking agents to drive outcomes.

6519
05:56:32,300 --> 05:56:36,780
And so obviously, we saw a lot of this outcome-based agentic development happen

6520
05:56:36,860 --> 05:56:38,540
when it comes to things like coding agents.

6521
05:56:38,540 --> 05:56:43,420
But, you know, as more and more of us internally started thinking about that beyond coding,

6522
05:56:43,420 --> 05:56:45,660
it felt like the chat box was a little bit limiting.

6523
05:56:45,660 --> 05:56:50,220
And so we started exploring, you know, all kinds of ways that we might want to chat and

6524
05:56:50,220 --> 05:56:54,300
work with agents and have them complete tasks and have access to systems that

6525
05:56:54,300 --> 05:56:56,060
really go beyond the chat box.

6526
05:56:56,060 --> 05:57:00,460
And, you know, the most common thing that kept coming up in all of these conversations

6527
05:57:00,460 --> 05:57:04,780
was that we kept sort of treating our agents as if they were colleagues.

6528
05:57:04,780 --> 05:57:08,300
We wanted to be able to interact with them as if they were sort of these smart

6529
05:57:08,300 --> 05:57:14,060
colleagues that sat next to us at our desks or were messageable in Slack or had access to

6530
05:57:14,060 --> 05:57:18,700
linear to look at tickets or, you know, wrote PRDs with us in Notion or whatever it is.

6531
05:57:18,700 --> 05:57:24,380
And so a lot of the motivation behind how RockBot was evolved and kind of why it is the way it is

6532
05:57:24,380 --> 05:57:28,940
today is grounded in this idea that, you know, we should really have these agentic

6533
05:57:28,940 --> 05:57:32,780
colleagues that we can work with on all the great things we do when we build products.

6534
05:57:32,780 --> 05:57:34,300
And so that was really the motivation.

6535
05:57:34,300 --> 05:57:39,340
So really, I think there's four things that we kind of took as a brief based on that

6536
05:57:39,340 --> 05:57:40,460
colleague definition.

6537
05:57:41,340 --> 05:57:46,940
The first one is that colleagues, when you work with them, often tie multiple tools together.

6538
05:57:46,940 --> 05:57:48,620
They don't live in one system.

6539
05:57:48,620 --> 05:57:50,460
They work across many systems.

6540
05:57:50,460 --> 05:57:52,140
And those systems do different things, right?

6541
05:57:52,140 --> 05:57:55,740
So you might have a ticketing system, like I mentioned, linear or something like it.

6542
05:57:55,740 --> 05:58:00,700
You might be using, you know, CRM or CRM++, you know, something like Salesforce or more.

6543
05:58:00,780 --> 05:58:04,460
You might be keeping documents in Notion, designs in Figma.

6544
05:58:04,460 --> 05:58:07,500
And all these tools are really, really great at what they do.

6545
05:58:07,500 --> 05:58:12,140
But oftentimes your colleagues or, you know, yourselves are tying things together across them.

6546
05:58:13,680 --> 05:58:18,480
The other things here are that these colleagues often have long running context.

6547
05:58:18,480 --> 05:58:22,160
They learn and they don't just, you know, work on a task,

6548
05:58:22,160 --> 05:58:23,760
eject the task and then move on.

6549
05:58:23,760 --> 05:58:26,800
The best colleagues you work with, the best teammates you work with,

6550
05:58:26,800 --> 05:58:28,800
they learn on the job, right?

6551
05:58:28,800 --> 05:58:31,600
Maybe they start out with very little company context.

6552
05:58:31,600 --> 05:58:34,640
You teach them some tasks or teach them how to get some work done.

6553
05:58:34,640 --> 05:58:35,600
They kind of do some work.

6554
05:58:35,600 --> 05:58:36,960
You give them some feedback, right?

6555
05:58:36,960 --> 05:58:38,240
That wasn't that great.

6556
05:58:38,240 --> 05:58:41,680
They hopefully take the feedback and then get better at their jobs.

6557
05:58:41,680 --> 05:58:45,200
We think that model generalizes to agents too, where agents can really,

6558
05:58:45,200 --> 05:58:48,240
you know, the way that the models have evolved, get good at learning,

6559
05:58:48,240 --> 05:58:51,920
at sort of building memory for themselves, at realizing what's important,

6560
05:58:51,920 --> 05:58:54,000
and at taking and incorporating that kind of feedback.

6561
05:58:55,410 --> 05:58:58,690
The third thing, and I think this is a really big one, is independence.

6562
05:58:58,930 --> 05:59:01,170
I think for a lot of, whoops, we lost the slides.

6563
05:59:01,170 --> 05:59:01,970
Let's go fix that.

6564
05:59:02,530 --> 05:59:03,010
There we go.

6565
05:59:03,810 --> 05:59:07,810
For a lot of people we work with, they go execute things independently, right?

6566
05:59:07,810 --> 05:59:09,730
Once in a while, maybe you'll get asked for,

6567
05:59:09,730 --> 05:59:11,810
hey, I need some help on blocking a task,

6568
05:59:11,810 --> 05:59:16,770
or, you know, maybe someone gets stuck with access to a system or something like this.

6569
05:59:17,730 --> 05:59:21,010
But really, the defining property of a colleague that you work with,

6570
05:59:21,010 --> 05:59:23,490
one of the defining properties is that they can work on their own,

6571
05:59:23,490 --> 05:59:27,010
they can complete tasks on their own, and they have their own access.

6572
05:59:27,010 --> 05:59:31,330
This is really what motivated the need for GrokBot to have its own computer.

6573
05:59:31,330 --> 05:59:34,610
We decided GrokBot should have its own computer that I can do things in.

6574
05:59:35,170 --> 05:59:37,250
This is true, and this is how the product works today.

6575
05:59:37,250 --> 05:59:40,370
If you go in and use GrokBot, GrokBot has its own computer,

6576
05:59:40,370 --> 05:59:43,010
you can kind of help it when it gets stuck on that computer,

6577
05:59:43,010 --> 05:59:45,730
but it has its own surface where it can create the output

6578
05:59:45,730 --> 05:59:47,810
that you need to get assisted in your work.

6579
05:59:49,140 --> 05:59:51,940
The last and final thing I'll call out here is messaging.

6580
05:59:51,940 --> 05:59:56,660
Earlier, I mentioned that it felt like we wanted to move beyond

6581
05:59:56,660 --> 05:59:59,940
the chat box a little bit, but I meant that a little bit tongue-in-cheek.

6582
06:00:00,000 --> 06:00:04,120
The chat box that defines how you interact with a large language of model.

6583
06:00:04,120 --> 06:00:07,840
We think that the core premise makes a lot of sense.

6584
06:00:07,840 --> 06:00:11,040
Where it started to feel a little bit constrained is that usually when you're chatting with a

6585
06:00:11,040 --> 06:00:12,920
colleague, you're like rapid-fire messaging.

6586
06:00:12,920 --> 06:00:16,160
If you're like me in Slack, or maybe like us in Slack, you're constantly streaming out

6587
06:00:16,160 --> 06:00:19,880
messages, you're switching between threads, you're multitasking.

6588
06:00:19,880 --> 06:00:24,320
We found that rather than having to wait your turn for the next response, that it's a lot

6589
06:00:24,320 --> 06:00:29,480
more engaging if you can just really jump in, interrupt, drive, and interact with agents

6590
06:00:29,480 --> 06:00:30,480
in this way.

6591
06:00:30,480 --> 06:00:31,480
You'll see a little bit of this in a minute.

6592
06:00:31,480 --> 06:00:35,320
But really, I think those are the four things that we found as we built this product and

6593
06:00:35,320 --> 06:00:38,280
played with it internally that define how colleagues work.

6594
06:00:38,280 --> 06:00:39,720
I love those four things.

6595
06:00:39,720 --> 06:00:41,000
It's not just like any colleague.

6596
06:00:41,000 --> 06:00:45,600
It's like really trying to encode the best colleagues that you have worked with.

6597
06:00:45,600 --> 06:00:49,320
They have a ton of context about the organization.

6598
06:00:49,320 --> 06:00:51,360
They understand and can learn on the job.

6599
06:00:51,360 --> 06:00:53,960
They can move independently, make their own decisions.

6600
06:00:53,960 --> 06:00:57,280
They're not constantly waiting for you.

6601
06:00:57,280 --> 06:01:01,600
And so yeah, I think this is like the high level of how we think about what GrokBot is.

6602
06:01:01,600 --> 06:01:07,140
It's an agent with a computer at the end of the day, and it feels like texting a colleague.

6603
06:01:07,140 --> 06:01:08,820
Exactly, yeah.

6604
06:01:08,820 --> 06:01:12,780
And I think there's two things here that if you want to sort of take away with you as

6605
06:01:12,780 --> 06:01:14,420
to what GrokBot can do.

6606
06:01:14,420 --> 06:01:16,260
It's these two things on the slide here.

6607
06:01:16,260 --> 06:01:19,740
We want these GrokBots to be AI teammates that you can get real work to and then come back

6608
06:01:19,740 --> 06:01:20,820
with results.

6609
06:01:20,820 --> 06:01:22,860
They don't come back with text, or they don't come back with questions.

6610
06:01:22,860 --> 06:01:24,340
They come back with results.

6611
06:01:24,340 --> 06:01:27,340
And then I think the last thing here is that they really finish jobs.

6612
06:01:27,340 --> 06:01:31,620
We think that really being able to get the job done is a much more productive way to

6613
06:01:31,620 --> 06:01:34,180
interact with agents and come back when they need approval.

6614
06:01:34,180 --> 06:01:36,420
So yeah, I'm curious, how do we use these with products?

6615
06:01:36,420 --> 06:01:37,420
Absolutely.

6616
06:01:37,420 --> 06:01:43,740
So we are actually going to see GrokBot in the wild, a real demo of some of the agents

6617
06:01:43,740 --> 06:01:47,340
that we have built, the colleagues that we work with in GrokBot.

6618
06:01:47,340 --> 06:01:51,340
I think there are really sort of three use cases that we're going to see here.

6619
06:01:51,340 --> 06:01:53,780
The first is what we call the attention list.

6620
06:01:53,780 --> 06:01:57,900
So as PMs, you're constantly pulled in a bunch of different directions.

6621
06:01:57,900 --> 06:02:03,180
Maybe you're working across a bunch of different projects, or there's a bunch of contexts everywhere.

6622
06:02:03,180 --> 06:02:07,740
And it is very helpful to think about what are you currently paying attention to?

6623
06:02:07,740 --> 06:02:12,480
What have you been paying attention to this morning, this week, this month?

6624
06:02:12,480 --> 06:02:16,740
And then really diff that against your priority list and help you understand how the ways

6625
06:02:16,740 --> 06:02:21,020
in which you're spending your time and where your focus is relative to what needs

6626
06:02:21,020 --> 06:02:23,630
to get done across the team.

6627
06:02:23,710 --> 06:02:28,070
And we're going to talk about how GrokBot can help you do that as this helpful assistant,

6628
06:02:28,070 --> 06:02:32,910
this colleague that's always on your shoulder, being able to learn about the ways in which

6629
06:02:32,910 --> 06:02:34,110
you're working.

6630
06:02:34,110 --> 06:02:37,590
The next is research and building up customer contacts.

6631
06:02:37,590 --> 06:02:42,350
Obviously, this is where a lot of product work goes into doing the research, figuring

6632
06:02:42,350 --> 06:02:48,630
out what users and customers actually need and want and love, what the biggest bottlenecks

6633
06:02:48,630 --> 06:02:53,510
are, what issues are they running into, how can you better serve them?

6634
06:02:53,590 --> 06:02:58,830
And so being able to gather up all that context, learn from your users, be able to synthesize

6635
06:02:58,830 --> 06:03:02,590
data in new ways, GrokBot is actually really phenomenal for.

6636
06:03:02,590 --> 06:03:07,310
And we're going to talk about some of the data science and analytics and product use

6637
06:03:07,310 --> 06:03:10,270
cases that we turn to GrokBot for.

6638
06:03:10,270 --> 06:03:11,670
And then, of course, shipping.

6639
06:03:11,670 --> 06:03:18,350
You cannot be a product manager in 2026 if you are not focused on delivering software

6640
06:03:18,350 --> 06:03:20,430
products to your customers.

6641
06:03:20,430 --> 06:03:26,310
And so really thinking about how GrokBot can not only accelerate you as a PM, but also

6642
06:03:26,310 --> 06:03:27,510
the entire team.

6643
06:03:27,510 --> 06:03:29,270
Yeah, there's a stat on the slide here.

6644
06:03:29,270 --> 06:03:33,430
GrokBot represents for us internally a double digit percentage of merged PRs.

6645
06:03:33,430 --> 06:03:35,390
And I think that's a pretty wild stat.

6646
06:03:35,390 --> 06:03:40,030
It also, within the product organization, empowers all of us as product people to be

6647
06:03:40,030 --> 06:03:42,350
able to ship PRs into production as well.

6648
06:03:42,350 --> 06:03:45,390
And so you just really get a bunch of superpowers when you can set up this in

6649
06:03:45,390 --> 06:03:46,830
the right way.

6650
06:03:46,830 --> 06:03:47,350
Very cool.

6651
06:03:47,350 --> 06:03:53,310
So let us, with that, meet the GrokBot team that we will be working with today.

6652
06:03:53,310 --> 06:03:57,310
Some familiar faces and names that we can all learn.

6653
06:03:57,310 --> 06:04:00,590
So first is Cora, Chief of Staff.

6654
06:04:00,590 --> 06:04:03,710
You can think of this as sort of the person that you turn to,

6655
06:04:03,710 --> 06:04:10,030
have access to your email, has access to your calendar, maybe listens on Slack for

6656
06:04:10,030 --> 06:04:15,990
some of the messages that you're sending, and really builds up a model of how you

6657
06:04:15,990 --> 06:04:20,300
as a PM actually work.

6658
06:04:20,300 --> 06:04:22,060
Next is Emily.

6659
06:04:22,060 --> 06:04:25,980
Emily is an engineering manager.

6660
06:04:25,980 --> 06:04:30,060
Emily has been coached not to actually do the coding, but

6661
06:04:30,060 --> 06:04:34,820
just to manage a team of engineers that we have given Emily.

6662
06:04:34,820 --> 06:04:42,020
And so Emily has five different engineering colleagues in GrokBot that she manages.

6663
06:04:42,020 --> 06:04:46,220
It's very helpful to have Emily as sort of this one contact across

6664
06:04:46,300 --> 06:04:52,060
the engineering team to be able to give larger chunks of work, have Emily then

6665
06:04:52,060 --> 06:04:57,180
deconstruct that and delegate that work, actually run the verification loops for

6666
06:04:57,180 --> 06:05:00,700
the outputs of some of the engineering agents.

6667
06:05:00,700 --> 06:05:03,940
We'll have Ashley, the go-to for data science, for

6668
06:05:03,940 --> 06:05:07,980
analytics, for being able to ask and answer data questions.

6669
06:05:07,980 --> 06:05:12,620
I think as a PM, oftentimes throughout the day, there is a random conversation

6670
06:05:12,620 --> 06:05:16,660
that sparks an idea that would have previously required you to go write

6671
06:05:16,660 --> 06:05:20,580
a bunch of SQL and figure out where the trusted data sources are.

6672
06:05:20,580 --> 06:05:23,900
And very helpful to have somebody that you can just quickly message and

6673
06:05:23,900 --> 06:05:29,660
get an answer, a chart, a synthesis of the underlying data set

6674
06:05:29,660 --> 06:05:33,360
that will help inform some of your decisions throughout the day.

6675
06:05:33,360 --> 06:05:34,880
We also have PM Pete.

6676
06:05:34,880 --> 06:05:39,480
PM Pete is a trusted sidekick for the product team,

6677
06:05:39,480 --> 06:05:43,280
being able to draft up some product requirement docs,

6678
06:05:43,280 --> 06:05:47,520
synthesize some of the customer and user insights, and

6679
06:05:47,520 --> 06:05:51,720
be able to run some of the lower level product work day to day.

6680
06:05:51,720 --> 06:05:53,790
You wanna talk about Pixel?

6681
06:05:53,790 --> 06:05:55,070
Yeah, Pixel's one of my favorites.

6682
06:05:55,070 --> 06:06:01,190
Pixel is a designer and so has been trained on some of the best advice for

6683
06:06:01,190 --> 06:06:04,190
how to be a world class AI designer.

6684
06:06:04,190 --> 06:06:07,990
It turns out that there are ways to make AI design look really good.

6685
06:06:07,990 --> 06:06:10,270
If you instruct your agents and bots in the right way.

6686
06:06:10,270 --> 06:06:14,430
And so Pixel's been loaded with a ton of context on how to be an S tier designer

6687
06:06:14,430 --> 06:06:17,860
from some of the best and latest thinking on the Internet.

6688
06:06:17,860 --> 06:06:20,820
Amazing, and finally we have Ray who is a recruiter.

6689
06:06:20,820 --> 06:06:23,380
So anybody who's in product leadership or

6690
06:06:23,380 --> 06:06:27,740
building out the product function or building out your company.

6691
06:06:27,740 --> 06:06:32,100
Very helpful to have a colleague that you can turn to on the people and

6692
06:06:32,100 --> 06:06:36,100
recruiting side of the house to help with some of the sourcing and

6693
06:06:36,100 --> 06:06:38,540
managing some of the hiring pipeline.

6694
06:06:38,540 --> 06:06:45,100
And so these are some of the very helpful Grokbot colleagues that we have defined.

6695
06:06:45,100 --> 06:06:49,580
But it is totally up to you exactly what your team is going to look like.

6696
06:06:49,580 --> 06:06:52,780
And so throughout the course of this demo, use this as inspiration,

6697
06:06:52,780 --> 06:06:54,380
use this for ideas.

6698
06:06:54,380 --> 06:06:57,380
But at the end of the day, you can construct your team,

6699
06:06:57,380 --> 06:07:01,380
figure out who is most helpful, what are the sets of roles that will be most

6700
06:07:01,380 --> 06:07:04,140
accelerative for you and your product team.

6701
06:07:04,140 --> 06:07:04,780
Yeah, definitely.

6702
06:07:04,780 --> 06:07:08,980
And I think to that point, what we've seen a lot of folks do is they create many

6703
06:07:08,980 --> 06:07:11,940
agents and so I wanted to spend a minute just talking about why we think that's

6704
06:07:11,940 --> 06:07:13,260
effective.

6705
06:07:13,260 --> 06:07:16,980
One is that I think while agents might have massive context windows that can

6706
06:07:16,980 --> 06:07:20,260
store entire novels, our brains, or at least my brain can't.

6707
06:07:20,260 --> 06:07:22,900
And so oftentimes we just wanna know who to reference and

6708
06:07:22,900 --> 06:07:26,500
it feels like a really natural pattern to assign roles to these agents

6709
06:07:26,500 --> 06:07:30,380
that correspond to how you'd interact with colleagues as we've talked about.

6710
06:07:30,380 --> 06:07:34,020
