7033
06:22:07,960 --> 06:22:09,940
Option A, clap.

7034
06:22:10,220 --> 06:22:15,190
Option B, clap.

7035
06:22:15,190 --> 06:22:16,030
Okay.

7036
06:22:16,030 --> 06:22:19,120
Sounds like team A.

7037
06:22:19,120 --> 06:22:24,120
All right, so let's hand the first mock over to Emily

7038
06:22:25,840 --> 06:22:28,720
to update the prototype.

7039
06:22:28,720 --> 06:22:29,560
Cool.

7040
06:22:29,560 --> 06:22:31,160
And what you're also noticing here is that

7041
06:22:31,160 --> 06:22:34,840
these bots can really act sort of interchangeably, right?

7042
06:22:34,840 --> 06:22:37,680
Like earlier it looked like Pixel had handed something

7043
06:22:37,680 --> 06:22:39,840
to Emily and then we made a decision

7044
06:22:39,840 --> 06:22:41,860
and then we can say, hey, like go and update Emily

7045
06:22:42,500 --> 06:22:43,420
and you'll notice that these bots

7046
06:22:43,420 --> 06:22:45,740
can sort of like interact more asynchronously

7047
06:22:45,740 --> 06:22:47,580
or more out of band than you might expect

7048
06:22:47,580 --> 06:22:49,260
if you're just used to working with

7049
06:22:49,260 --> 06:22:50,980
more of a chat style interface.

7050
06:22:50,980 --> 06:22:52,740
And honestly, I think these make a lot more fun

7051
06:22:52,740 --> 06:22:55,100
to work with where basically you can continue to steer

7052
06:22:55,100 --> 06:22:57,780
and drive what the agents are working on.

7053
06:22:57,780 --> 06:22:59,380
Particularly in some cases where they're working

7054
06:22:59,380 --> 06:23:01,140
on something for a few minutes at a time,

7055
06:23:01,140 --> 06:23:03,860
kind of getting that input from you as you go

7056
06:23:03,860 --> 06:23:05,500
kind of helps you feel like you're being a part

7057
06:23:05,500 --> 06:23:08,600
of the process and also verifying the output too.

7058
06:23:08,600 --> 06:23:11,880
Cool, so let's check in on the engineering team.

7059
06:23:12,680 --> 06:23:16,400
You can see Emily here as the engineering manager

7060
06:23:16,400 --> 06:23:19,640
deconstructed the different priorities

7061
06:23:19,640 --> 06:23:24,640
into scoped work for each one of the engineers

7062
06:23:24,840 --> 06:23:28,360
and actually then went and had direct conversations

7063
06:23:28,360 --> 06:23:32,480
with each one explaining the work that they were to do

7064
06:23:32,480 --> 06:23:36,480
and also included a bunch of additional context.

7065
06:23:36,480 --> 06:23:38,880
One of the things that I think we have all learned

7066
06:23:38,880 --> 06:23:42,240
the hard way is that agents are really good at prompting.

7067
06:23:42,240 --> 06:23:45,280
Oftentimes better than we are at figuring out

7068
06:23:45,280 --> 06:23:47,720
what context to give to an agent.

7069
06:23:47,720 --> 06:23:52,720
And so oftentimes just giving your team in GrokBot

7070
06:23:53,240 --> 06:23:55,480
the context of what you were trying to achieve

7071
06:23:55,480 --> 06:23:57,520
and letting them work together and suss out

7072
06:23:57,520 --> 06:23:59,360
what context would be most helpful,

7073
06:23:59,360 --> 06:24:01,880
how to deconstruct work in different ways

7074
06:24:01,880 --> 06:24:04,110
can be very accelerated.

7075
06:24:04,110 --> 06:24:08,670
One thing we will hopefully see in action a second here.

7076
06:24:08,670 --> 06:24:11,270
These GrokBots also have the ability,

7077
06:24:11,270 --> 06:24:13,030
there we go, to kick off cloud agents.

7078
06:24:13,030 --> 06:24:15,310
And so when it comes to the actual engineering work,

7079
06:24:15,310 --> 06:24:17,550
what each of these IC engineers are doing

7080
06:24:17,550 --> 06:24:19,830
is they're creating agents in the cloud

7081
06:24:19,830 --> 06:24:22,950
that sort of run with a local copy of your code repository.

7082
06:24:22,950 --> 06:24:26,350
So when it comes time to make changes to the code base

7083
06:24:26,350 --> 06:24:29,930
or maybe they need to install some setup scripts

7084
06:24:29,930 --> 06:24:32,030
to be able to ensure that the repo is testable

7085
06:24:32,030 --> 06:24:34,310
and runnable, these agents come with all

7086
06:24:34,310 --> 06:24:35,590
of those batteries included too.

7087
06:24:35,590 --> 06:24:38,510
So in this case, it looks like you just said yes

7088
06:24:39,230 --> 06:24:42,150
when Nova asked about creating a PR, is that right?

7089
06:24:42,150 --> 06:24:44,070
That's right, yeah.

7090
06:24:44,070 --> 06:24:46,830
And so Nova has gone off, launched this cloud agent,

7091
06:24:46,830 --> 06:24:51,370
will continue to monitor its work when that completes.

7092
06:24:51,370 --> 06:24:54,490
You can also have an additional verification loop.

7093
06:24:54,490 --> 06:24:57,150
The engineer can review the work of the cloud agent,

7094
06:24:57,150 --> 06:24:59,790
can hand it back to either a QA team

7095
06:24:59,790 --> 06:25:03,090
or your engineering manager who can actually run

7096
06:25:03,090 --> 06:25:06,390
an additional layer of checks and verification

7097
06:25:06,390 --> 06:25:09,870
to ensure that the outputs align with the intended goals

7098
06:25:09,870 --> 06:25:12,550
that you gave your team to start.

7099
06:25:12,550 --> 06:25:15,270
Yeah, I think what's really cool about this too

7100
06:25:15,270 --> 06:25:17,030
is you can sort of choose how much human

7101
06:25:17,030 --> 06:25:18,370
you want to bring into the loop, right?

7102
06:25:18,370 --> 06:25:19,950
So maybe for some of these cases,

7103
06:25:19,950 --> 06:25:23,070
if it's a really easy change to say a documentation website

7104
06:25:23,070 --> 06:25:26,510
or maybe it's resizing something in our mobile app,

7105
06:25:26,510 --> 06:25:28,150
it feels like a pretty low stakes change

7106
06:25:28,150 --> 06:25:30,310
and sometimes we can just let our agents run with it.

7107
06:25:30,310 --> 06:25:32,070
But perhaps there's a more meaningful change

7108
06:25:32,070 --> 06:25:34,210
if we wanna make sure that this design was implemented

7109
06:25:34,210 --> 06:25:35,510
the way that we spec'd it out

7110
06:25:35,510 --> 06:25:37,430
or the way that Pixel helped us spec'd it out,

7111
06:25:37,430 --> 06:25:39,990
we might wanna jump in and ask for a demo or prototype.

7112
06:25:39,990 --> 06:25:42,870
And so one thing we found that's just generally true

7113
06:25:42,870 --> 06:25:45,590
when working with agents is that giving them an environment

7114
06:25:45,590 --> 06:25:48,130
to verify their own output in makes a huge difference,

7115
06:25:48,130 --> 06:25:51,590
but also giving them the tools to help you as the human

7116
06:25:51,590 --> 06:25:54,430
verify the output also makes a huge difference in outcomes.

7117
06:25:54,430 --> 06:25:56,230
And you can kind of work with those things together

7118
06:25:56,230 --> 06:26:00,180
and get better results, so.

7119
06:26:00,180 --> 06:26:02,280
Amazing, so I think our engineering team

7120
06:26:02,280 --> 06:26:05,420
is gonna cook on this in the background.

7121
06:26:05,420 --> 06:26:07,220
And so what have we seen so far?

7122
06:26:07,220 --> 06:26:11,460
We started with this open question about the business.

7123
06:26:11,460 --> 06:26:16,260
We started talking to a data science colleague, Ashley,

7124
06:26:16,260 --> 06:26:19,060
to help us better understand some of the funnel dynamics

7125
06:26:19,060 --> 06:26:20,940
where the biggest fall off was,

7126
06:26:20,940 --> 06:26:25,940
handed all of that context over to a PMP agent

7127
06:26:26,620 --> 06:26:30,400
who could actually help write the product requirements doc.

7128
06:26:30,400 --> 06:26:31,800
That was the first pass.

7129
06:26:31,800 --> 06:26:34,420
We then handed it off to our designer

7130
06:26:34,420 --> 06:26:37,060
who created a first version of the mocks

7131
06:26:37,060 --> 06:26:38,500
to actually go and implement.

7132
06:26:38,500 --> 06:26:41,860
And then Emily and our engineering team are off now

7133
06:26:41,860 --> 06:26:43,900
cooking it with our cloud agents.

7134
06:26:45,040 --> 06:26:47,160
Really cool system, and again, you can really adapt this

7135
06:26:47,160 --> 06:26:49,200
to whatever workflow makes the most sense to your business.

7136
06:26:49,200 --> 06:26:51,440
And so we've seen a lot of folks who wanna go

7137
06:26:51,440 --> 06:26:53,400
really deep on the requirements phase

7138
06:26:53,400 --> 06:26:55,360
and maybe work on really complex systems

7139
06:26:55,360 --> 06:26:57,840
where there's lots of knock on implications

7140
06:26:57,840 --> 06:26:59,760
for every small requirement.

7141
06:26:59,760 --> 06:27:02,480
You could imagine having a team of folks whose job it is

7142
06:27:02,480 --> 06:27:04,760
to make sure there's a knowledge base that's come up to date

7143
06:27:04,760 --> 06:27:07,360
and that those requirements are fed into the product.

7144
06:27:07,360 --> 06:27:09,860
Or maybe you have a really convoluted code base

7145
06:27:09,860 --> 06:27:12,020
with a bunch of different repos that need to stay in sync.

7146
06:27:12,020 --> 06:27:13,560
That's another workflow that you could build in

7147
06:27:13,560 --> 06:27:14,400
with GrokBot.

7148
06:27:14,400 --> 06:27:16,000
And so my point with that is just stating that

7149
06:27:16,000 --> 06:27:18,740
this is one example of how you might set up your team,

7150
06:27:18,740 --> 06:27:20,420
but really the tool is meant to be flexible

7151
06:27:20,420 --> 06:27:22,680
for you to adapt to whatever workflow works

7152
06:27:22,680 --> 06:27:33,150
for your own organization.

7153
06:27:33,150 --> 06:27:33,990
Cool.

7154
06:27:33,990 --> 06:27:36,990
Okay, now a few of the hard learned lessons

7155
06:27:36,990 --> 06:27:39,270
working with GrokBot, a few things to keep in mind

7156
06:27:39,270 --> 06:27:43,670
as you start to build out your own teams in GrokBot.

7157
06:27:43,670 --> 06:27:47,310
First is this idea of named agents having separate memory,

7158
06:27:47,310 --> 06:27:49,450
being able to learn on the job,

7159
06:27:49,450 --> 06:27:52,070
and really thinking about these as colleagues.

7160
06:27:52,070 --> 06:27:54,990
They're not going to be great on day zero.

7161
06:27:54,990 --> 06:27:58,130
There is an onboarding phase where you can give them skills,

7162
06:27:58,130 --> 06:28:01,030
you can give them context, you can teach them things.

7163
06:28:01,030 --> 06:28:03,950
And so over time, they actually build up that context

7164
06:28:03,950 --> 06:28:06,590
and intuition and can take on more and more work,

7165
06:28:06,590 --> 06:28:08,410
which is cool to see.

7166
06:28:08,410 --> 06:28:12,290
The other is really thinking about sort of reducing noise

7167
06:28:12,290 --> 06:28:15,290
in the system as PMs, like there is often a lot of pings,

7168
06:28:15,290 --> 06:28:17,330
a lot of emails, a lot of slacks.

7169
06:28:17,330 --> 06:28:20,090
One of the pro tips that we've learned is

7170
06:28:20,090 --> 06:28:22,290
when you set up a routine to ask your agent,

7171
06:28:22,290 --> 06:28:24,090
if this is essentially a no operation,

7172
06:28:24,090 --> 06:28:27,010
if there's nothing important or urgent for me to see,

7173
06:28:27,010 --> 06:28:29,290
you can actually just take the work on yourself

7174
06:28:29,290 --> 06:28:31,370
or you don't need to update me.

7175
06:28:31,370 --> 06:28:35,210
And so there is a bit of noise reduction in the system.

7176
06:28:35,210 --> 06:28:40,210
Agents are, and Grokbot is currently grooming my inbox

7177
06:28:40,250 --> 06:28:44,050
and will only tell me about the most important emails there

7178
06:28:44,050 --> 06:28:45,610
and can escalate things,

7179
06:28:45,610 --> 06:28:49,850
but generally I'm trying to reduce the noise in the system

7180
06:28:49,850 --> 06:28:52,290
so I can keep focused on what is most important

7181
06:28:52,290 --> 06:28:53,410
for the team.

7182
06:28:53,410 --> 06:28:55,330
And then agents all the way down,

7183
06:28:55,330 --> 06:28:59,410
this idea that you can have managers of agents,

7184
06:28:59,410 --> 06:29:02,490
agents are coordinating with cloud agents,

7185
06:29:02,490 --> 06:29:05,610
and Grokbot is a very helpful agent system

7186
06:29:05,610 --> 06:29:08,410
for helping you take on some of the most ambitious work

7187
06:29:08,410 --> 06:29:10,010
and accelerate your product teams.

7188
06:29:10,010 --> 06:29:14,310
Yeah, very cool.

